# These test bins are USER-side leaves: each one names its message crates as
# path deps into `generated/`, which `nros generate-rust` produces from the
# HOST's message packages (`scripts/regenerate-bindings.sh` section 3, run by
# `just generate-bindings` — a dependency of `build-test-fixtures`, so the
# build stage recreates them before anything is compiled).
#
# They used to be committed, which made a fresh clone look like it had them
# while the contents belonged to whoever last regenerated.
/*/generated/

# Every bin's `Cargo.lock` is SEEDED, never committed (phase-485 W2, RFC-0104
# D3). A lock is tracked only where its directory is in the index's
# `[rust] locked_roots`, and no bin is one; the fixture builder seeds each from
# the registry pin set (`nros ws seed-lock`) on its first build, and
# `check-cargo-registry-pins` refuses a tracked leaf lock. So the rule is now a
# blanket one. It used to be a list, because some bins kept tracked locks and a
# blanket rule would have made those unaddable; none do any more.
/*/Cargo.lock

# `nros sync` / `nros codegen-system` write per-leaf build output here:
# `build/nros/providers.json` and `build/nros/models/<bringup>/…`. SystemModels
# are BUILD ARTIFACTS and never committed (phase-330 W4.a, gated by
# `check-no-tracked-models`), and the provider index is regenerated by every
# build that reads it.
#
# Blanket, unlike the `Cargo.lock` list above, and for the opposite reason:
# nothing under a leaf's `build/` is ever legitimately tracked (verified: zero
# tracked paths match), so there is no correct entry a blanket rule could
# swallow. Mirrors the repo-root `examples/**/build/` line.
/*/build/
